course: dictionary
lesson: 14534
title: Keep going: the word persist
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To persist means to keep doing something even when it is hard, or when other people want you to stop. «Maya kept trying to tie her shoelace, even though it kept coming undone.» Maya persists. She does not give up.
explain: Persist also works for bad things. If something bad persists, it carries on for longer than you expected. «If the cough persists, see a doctor» is common advice on medicine packets, because it means the cough has not gone away.
explain: Look for clue words such as even though, still, and again. They often show that someone is persisting or that something is lasting. Persist is a verb, so its forms are persists, persisted and persisting: «Tom persisted with his maths problem until he solved it.»
fact: To persist means to keep doing something even when it is hard.
fact: «Persist» can also mean that something bad keeps going for longer than expected.
fact: Common phrases are persist in, persist with, and if symptoms persist.
fact: Use the right form: persists (now), persisted (before), and persisting (still going on).
fact: A pupil who persists keeps trying a hard problem instead of giving up.
exercise: «Lily persisted with her painting, even when it went wrong twice.» What does persisted mean? | *Kept going | Forgot about it | Gave up | Got bored and went home
exercise: «If the headache persists, ask a grown-up to call the doctor.» What does persists mean here? | Moves to a different place | *Continues for longer than expected | Gets smaller and disappears | Goes away quickly
exercise: «Sam practised his tricky song again and again, persisting until he got every note right.» Find and copy the word that shows Sam kept trying. | *persisting | tricky | practised | right
test: «Ella ___ asking questions until she understood the answer.» Which word fills the gap? | Stopped | Refused | *Persisted | Forgot
test: On a medicine leaflet, what does «if symptoms persist» mean? | *If the signs of illness do not go away | If the signs go away quickly | If the medicine has run out | If you feel very happy
exam: Which sentence uses persist correctly? | *Ben persisted with the hard puzzle until he solved it. | Ben persisted in sleeping all day. | The puzzle persisted in the box. | Ben persist the puzzle yesterday.
